module Darklang.Cli.Docs.CLI

let content () : String =
  """# CLI Commands

`help` lists every command, grouped. This is the ones you reach for, with a
sentence of context each; `docs scm` is the one to read for how source control
WORKS, because that model is not git's.

## The four words

Four ideas, one word each, so the docs and the output agree:

  item       one fn, type or value. Content-addressed, so anything with the
             same body IS the same item, shared by everyone who wrote it
  location   a name bound to an item: owner, module path, name. Branch-scoped,
             which is what lets two branches disagree about what a name means
  op         one recorded change. The log of them IS the program; everything
             else is folded from it
  matter     all of it -- items plus locations, folded from ops -- and the
             substrate that stores and moves them

`packages` means the published set, not the content. `entity` is not a word we
use; it was a fifth name for `item`.

## Global flags
  --branch <name>   run ONE command on a branch, without switching to it
  --safe            ignore the stored entry point, run the shipped CLI
  --json            on any command that answers a question

Branch context DOES persist: `dark switch <name>` moves you and the next `dark`
starts there. `--branch` is for a one-off.

## Writing code
  fn <name> "<body>"      create or update a function
  type <name> "<body>"    create or update a type
  val <name> "<expr>"     create or update a value (`let` is an alias)
  module <path> -         several declarations at once, from stdin
  edit <name> [<file>]    apply a file, or open $EDITOR on the declaration
  typecheck               audit the declarations on this branch
  deprecate / delete      take something off the shelf (see: docs deprecation)
  undeprecate <name>      put it back

Your edits are LIVE as soon as you write them. There is no build step.

## Running
  eval <expr>       evaluate an expression -- this is how you call a function,
                    e.g. `eval MyMod.myFn 1 2`
  run-script <f>    run a .dark script file (`run` is an alias)
  scripts           store and re-run named scripts
  traces            what ran, and what it returned

## Looking around
  nav <path>        go to a module (`cd`)
  ls / back / tree  list / go back / show the hierarchy
  view <name>       inspect a fn, type or value
  search <term>     find anything
  deps <name>       what it uses and what uses it (`--json`, `--deep`)
  builtins          the F# builtins
  find-values       every value of a given type

## Your changes
  status            what changed, what FOLLOWED your change, what's waiting
  commit "<msg>"    review your draft and commit it (`-y` skips the prompt)
  commits           the commits, newest first
  show <id>         what a commit committed, or what one op does
  discard           drop the whole draft
  undo <name>       step ONE item back a version
  propagate         choose what follows what when a dependency changes
  conflicts         review what a merge, rebase or sync auto-resolved
  constraints       standing checks over the store
  ack <id>...       leave a constraint finding for now (`ack --all`)
  ops               read this branch's op log

## Branches
  branch            which branch am I on
  branch <name>     switch to it, starting it if it's new
  branches          list them (name, short id, parent, status)
  switch <name>     same as `branch <name>`; `switch main` returns
  diff <name>       what it changes vs its parent
  log [<name>]      its ops (on main: the commits)
  rebase <name>     take the parent's current state
  merge <name> [-y] fold it into its parent
  resolve <branch> <name> mine|theirs
  (see: docs scm)

## Sync
  connect <url>     remember a relay and push (once per machine)
  sync              push then pull -- what you run day to day
  push / pull       one direction
  whoami            this instance's sync identity (`identity <name>` renames it)
  review            stage someone else's ops instead of applying them
  backups           copies of the store: what exists, take one, prune
  (see: docs scm)

## Serving and apps
  serve <router> [--port N]   an HTTP server backed by a Darklang router
                              (alias: http-server; --port defaults to 8080)
  apps              list, install and manage apps (daemons, foreground, UI)
  devices           your tailnet devices (status/serve/ping)

## The full-screen experience (`dark` with no args opens it)
  workbench         the framed, navigable workbench (alias: wb)
  views             browse + preview the individual CLI views
  outliner          the interactive tree outliner

## Account, config, and the rest
  install / update / uninstall / version   the `dark` binary itself
  login [name]      log in (no name lists accounts); persists across runs
  logout            clear the active account
  config            per-install settings (`config set <key> <value>`)
  caps              what this instance is allowed to do
  agent <verb>      AI workflows: ask/code/review/fix/diff (alias: ai)
  help [cmd] / docs [topic] / clear / quit

## Troubleshooting
If something hangs or behaves unexpectedly, check the logs:
  rundir/logs/cli.log    # main CLI log
  rundir/logs/           # other logs (parser, etc.)"""
